  "image_name": "1f0ccfcdde07ee0b123c9bfc4700b8ca.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a high-salt, sodium-rich environment appears to shift immune cells toward inflammation. Across all four schematics, Na+ surrounds different immune cell types and is linked to signaling changes that favor pro-inflammatory responses while suppressing anti-inflammatory functions.\n\nIn T helper cells, sodium promotes a Th17-inducing state through pathways involving p38 MAPK, NFAT5, SGK1, and RORγt, leading to greater Th17 differentiation and IL-17 production. In contrast, regulatory T cells are shown in a Treg-inducing setting but with reduced activity, centered on FOXP3, and accompanied by lower anti-inflammatory output, with cytokine changes indicating less IL-10 and more IFNγ.\n\nThe lower half shows a similar pattern in macrophages. In classically activated M1 macrophages, sodium is associated with ROS generation, inflammasome activation, and signaling through p38 MAPK, NFAT5, and NF-κB, resulting in release of nitric oxide, IL-1β, and other pro-inflammatory mediators. Meanwhile, alternatively activated M2 macrophages show impaired mitochondrial function and altered AKT, mTOR, ERK1, and STAT6 signaling, corresponding to decreased anti-inflammatory activity.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that excess sodium skews both T cells and macrophages toward inflammation.",
